  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nLow stratus, fog and light patchy drizzle continue early this \nmorning across parts of the forecast area. The areas that seem to be \nthe hardest hit are across northeast SD and the Prairie Coteau \nregion where sfc obs and area webcams confirm the lower visibilities \ndue to the fog and the drizzle. May need to consider a statement or \na headline in the near future if conditions continue to worsen. \nElsewhere, the fog and light drizzle are more patchy across west \ncentral MN as well as areas along and west of the James Valley. By \nmid to late morning, the fog and lower visibilities will improve \nacross central and parts of northeast SD and west central MN. \nHowever, would anticipate the low stratus and patchy fog to persist \nacross the Prairie Coteau and the I-29 corridor into the afternoon. \nVisibilities should improve by then with the possible exception of \nthe Summit area.\n\nSfc analysis indicates a couple areas of low pressure within the \nregion. The first one is located around Sioux City, IA and was the \nmain culprit for our rain and drizzle on Friday. This system is \nprogged to continue its trek south and east and away from our area. \nThe second low pressure system is located in northeast WY around \nGillette. It had been aiding some late evening showers and \nthunderstorms across western SD and the Black Hills region. That \nactivity has either fizzled as it crossed through the northern Hills \nor dived southeast into NE where it has persisted. Weak shortwave \nridging is expected across parts of the central and eastern Dakotas \ntoday which should help to keep most zones dry. Low level northeast \nflow should effectively maintain overcast skies underneath this \nridging. Cooler than normal temps, mainly in the 60s are expected \nthrough the afternoon. An upper level low currently sits across the \nGreat Basin. A shortwave trough rotating around this system will \ntraverse northeast across WY into western SD today. This will \nprovide the energy needed to generate more scattered showers across \nwestern SD. Some of these light showers could work into central \nsections of SD this afternoon. An increasing chance for more \nwidespread precipitation is expected by Saturday evening into Sunday \nacross the western half of SD as the upper level low rotates through \nWY tonight and into western SD and the NE Panhandle Sunday \nafternoon into early Monday. Rain chances get about as far east the \nJames Valley late Sunday into early Monday before all the activity \nshifts south as the upper low slides south and east into NE on \nMonday and Monday night.\n\nGuidance progs indicate that by late Monday into Tuesday we'll see a \nbreak from the precipitation has upper ridging briefly builds \noverhead. This may be our first widespread opportunity to see a \nbreak in the cloud cover. Southwest flow aloft will resume by \nWednesday into Thursday. Another shortwave trough is expected to \ntrack northeast into parts of the Northern Plains on Wednesday into \nThursday. This system will result in another chance for showers. \nTemperatures will begin to modify by this time frame with readings \nclimbing back closer to late September normals. After the upper wave \npasses through by the end of the week, upper ridging is expected \nwhich will lead to a drier and warmer trend for the area.",
